  Which brings up the second thing that matters, and it's the part with no existing structure.

  [1mThe fork:[0m what "track" means concretely — where a login record goes. Right now the only output in the entire repo is
  [38;5;153mconsole.log[39m, so anything beyond that is new.

  One constraint worth naming first: [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is a plain browser script loaded via [38;5;153m<script src="app.js">[39m with no bundler
  and no module system, while [38;5;153msrc/[39m uses CommonJS for Node. They don't currently share code, and there's no path by which
  they could without adding tooling. So a "tracking module" in the browser means a second [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag and a global,
  not a [38;5;153mrequire[39m.

  - [1mKeep it at [38;5;153mconsole.log[0m — extend the existing log line to include the userId. Buys honesty: the codebase is a stub
    that says so in a comment, and this keeps the same fidelity. Costs: "track" doesn't really mean anything yet. Rules
    out nothing; trivially replaced later.
  - [1mA small tracking module with one seam[0m — [38;5;153mtracking.js[39m, loaded before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, exposing something like [38;5;153mtrackLoginEvent({
[39m    [38;5;153muserId, success, timestamp })[39m that logs today and has a single obvious place to plug in a real sink. Buys a named
    boundary, so later work changes one function instead of hunting call sites. Costs: a new file, a new script tag, and
    a global. This is the new structure the request implies.
